1:{title:'Read the World',body:`Setting is more than where and when. AP readers notice four overlapping layers of the world an author builds.`,chips:[
 ['Physical / Natural','Geography, climate, weather, elements, flora, fauna, and natural resources.'],
 ['Manufactured','Buildings, rooms, roads, transportation, technology, architecture, and human-made spaces.'],
 ['Temporal / Historical','The time-world: past, present, future, or imagined; laws, events, institutions, technology, season, and time of day.'],
 ['Social / Cultural','Gender roles, class roles, family expectations, customs, religion, politics, hierarchy, accepted behavior, and power.']
],note:'The strongest details often overlap. A staircase can be manufactured setting and also reveal a social hierarchy.'},
