const P4A=`The train station had been built for crowds, but at four in the morning it belonged to echoes. Celia sat beneath a clock that had stopped at 11:17, though every digital board above the platforms continued to flash ON TIME. Beyond the glass doors, snow had buried the taxi lane and erased the curb. Inside, the vending machines hummed beside rows of bolted metal seats. A security guard crossed the concourse once every ten minutes, never looking directly at her. Celia kept her suitcase between her shoes. Her brother's message—DON'T GET ON—glowed on her phone until the battery warning covered it. When the loudspeaker announced Track Six, the voice was calm enough to make departure sound inevitable.`;
const P4B=`Every house on Bellweather Street had the same porch, the same narrow lawn, and the same maple planted exactly twelve feet from the curb. In October the leaves turned together, as if the trees had received instructions. Mrs. Vale liked to say the neighborhood was “orderly.” Nia's mother called it “watched.” The homeowners' association had mailed three notices about the blue bicycle chained to their railing and one about the small vegetable garden beside the driveway. On Saturday morning, Nia found a fourth envelope tucked beneath the brass knocker. Through the front window she could see Mr. Harlan across the street trimming a hedge already level with his windowsill. He lifted two fingers in greeting without stopping the shears. Nia carried the letter to the kitchen, where her mother was packing jars of tomatoes into a cardboard box. “Don't throw them out,” Nia said. Her mother folded the notice once, then again. Outside, a landscaping truck moved slowly down the street, spraying the identical lawns with a chemical mist.`;
const P4C=`When the tide withdrew from Kestrel Island, it revealed a road no map acknowledged. The stones began at the ruined chapel and ran across the flats toward the mainland, black with seaweed and polished by centuries of water. Mara's grandfather had warned her never to cross after the third bell, because the tide returned faster than a person could run. The islanders repeated the warning so often that children learned it before they learned the names of the months. At dusk, shutters closed along the harbor. Nets came down. Even the tavern sign was carried indoors. Mara had once believed the ritual was about weather. Then, during her eighteenth winter, the mainland ferry stopped coming. The council said the channel was unsafe. Her mother said nothing. For six weeks the island ate from salt barrels and root cellars while lanterns burned in the council house long after midnight. On the seventh week, Mara found fresh wheel tracks leading from the ruined chapel to the exposed road. The third bell sounded. Across the flats, a lantern answered from the mainland.`;

const FINAL_PASSAGE=`By the time the reservoir swallowed the old town, everyone had already moved uphill. The church steeple was the last thing to disappear. For years afterward, when summer lowered the water, its brass cross returned first, then the roofline, then the black mouths of windows that no longer opened into rooms. Tourists came to photograph the ruins from the overlook. They bought lemonade from the new visitor center and read a plaque explaining that the valley had been “successfully repurposed for regional growth.”\n\nLena's father refused to use the overlook. He drove instead to the maintenance road below the dam, where warning signs leaned against the fence and cottonwoods grew through cracks in the asphalt. From there, the water looked less like a lake than a wall. “Your grandmother's kitchen is under that,” he once told Lena, pointing toward a smooth blue surface broken only by wind.\n\nAt seventeen, Lena began working summers at the visitor center. She learned to say “historic relocation” instead of “eviction” and “water security” instead of “the dam.” Most visitors wanted to know whether fish lived inside the submerged houses. Lena told them the houses had been stripped before flooding. She did not tell them that her grandfather had nailed his front door shut and made the sheriff remove him through a window.\n\nLate in August, the drought became severe enough that the steeple rose again. This time the roof followed. Then the upper floor of the schoolhouse appeared, its brick walls streaked white with mineral lines. People gathered along the railings with cameras. Lena's supervisor set out a sandwich board: RARE HISTORIC VIEW—ASK US ABOUT THE LOST TOWN.\n\nAfter closing, Lena walked beyond the official overlook. The fence had been cut near the maintenance road. Below, mud stretched toward the ruins in a cracked plain. Her father was already there. He stood beside the exposed road that once led into town, holding a small tin sign Lena recognized from family photographs: PINE STREET.\n\n“You shouldn't take that,” she said.\n\nHe rubbed the mud from one corner. “They already took the street.”\n\nBehind them, turbines hummed inside the dam. Above them, the visitor center lights clicked on one by one.`;

